<h1>7.3 Cache avalanche</h1>
<p class="lead">A stampede is many requests for <b>one</b> key. An avalanche is <b>many keys</b> missing at the same time, so a large part of all traffic suddenly falls through to the database.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="7.3"></div>

<div class="rw-head"><h2>&#127757; In the real world</h2></div>
<p class="rw-sub">How the biggest companies on the internet meet the idea from this lesson.</p>
<div class="rw-grid">
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#1877f2">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">📘</span>Facebook</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Sept 2010: ~2.5 hours down<small>a cache-driven feedback loop</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">An automated system pushed an invalid configuration value. Every client saw it in the cache, treated it as invalid and went to the database at the same time. The databases were flooded with hundreds of thousands of queries per second, and Facebook had to take the site down to stop the loop.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> When everything misses at once, the database cannot survive.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: Facebook engineering post, &quot;More Details on Today&#x27;s Outage&quot;, 2010</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#1877f2">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">📘</span>Facebook</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Cold cluster warmup<small>never start from empty</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">When Facebook brings up a new memcache cluster, it first fills misses from a warm cluster instead of the database, so the new, empty cache does not hammer the databases.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Warm caches gradually instead of letting live traffic hit the source.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: &quot;Scaling Memcache at Facebook&quot;, NSDI 2013</div>
    </div>
</div>



<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>Two common causes</h2>
<div class="story mini" data-story="7.3-restart"></div>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>Synchronized expiry</b>
        <p class="muted">A job warms 10,000 entries at 09:00 with a 1-hour TTL. At 10:00 all 10,000 expire in the same second, and the next requests all miss together.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <b>Cache outage or restart</b>
        <p class="muted">The Redis server restarts, fails over, or a deploy clears every in-memory cache. Everything is cold at once.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Fixes</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Fix</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>TTL jitter</b></td><td>Add a random amount to each TTL (for example 1 hour &plusmn; 10%) so expiries spread out over time.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Gradual warm-up</b></td><td>After a restart, warm the most important keys first and gradually, instead of letting live traffic do it all at once.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Multi-level caching</b></td><td>An L1 in each server (lesson 7.4) absorbs traffic while L2 refills.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Highly available cache</b></td><td>Replicas and failover (lesson 5.4), so one node's failure does not empty the cache.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Protect the database</b></td><td>Single flight per key (7.1), a limit on concurrent database loads, and a circuit breaker that serves stale data or a friendly error instead of overloading the database.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Jitter is one line of code:</p>
<pre><code>var spread = (Random.Shared.NextDouble() * 2 - 1) * ttlSeconds * jitterPercent / 100.0;
cache.Set(key, value, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(ttlSeconds + spread));</code></pre>

</details>

<h2><span class="try-it">Try it</span>Live demo</h2>
<p>The button caches 200 entries twice: once with a fixed 20-second TTL, once with 20 seconds &plusmn; 50%. The charts show how many entries expire in each second. Then watch the live counters as entries really expire.</p>
<div class="card">
    <div class="toolbar">
        <button class="btn primary" id="btnWarm">Cache 200 entries both ways</button>
        <span id="elapsed" class="muted"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div>
            <b>Fixed TTL (20 s)</b>
            <div id="chart-fixed" style="display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:2px;height:140px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"></div>
            <p class="muted" id="peak-fixed"></p>
            <div class="stat"><div class="value" id="alive-fixed">-</div><div class="label">entries still cached</div></div>
        </div>
        <div>
            <b>With jitter (20 s &plusmn; 50%)</b>
            <div id="chart-jitter" style="display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:2px;height:140px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"></div>
            <p class="muted" id="peak-jitter"></p>
            <div class="stat"><div class="value" id="alive-jitter">-</div><div class="label">entries still cached</div></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">The x-axis runs from 0 to 30 seconds after caching. Bar height: entries expiring in that second, so database loads in that second if all of them are requested again.</p>
</div>

<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>The cliff.</b> Click the button. The fixed chart has one bar: all 200 entries expire in second 20. The jitter chart spreads them from second 10 to 30, with a peak of only about 10 per second.</li>
    <li><b>Watch it happen.</b> Keep watching the counters. At about 20 seconds the fixed counter drops from 200 to 0 at once. The jittered one drains gradually from second 10 to 30.</li>
    <li><b>Think in database load.</b> If each expired entry is requested once right after expiring, fixed TTL sends 200 queries in one second; jitter sends about 10 per second for 20 seconds. Same total work, but a database can handle the second shape.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Avoid synchronized expiry by adding jitter to TTLs, especially for entries cached in bulk. Plan for a cold cache: warm up gradually, keep an L1, run the cache with replicas, and protect the database with single flight, concurrency limits and circuit breakers.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Bulk-loading thousands of keys with the same TTL at startup or from a nightly job. Assuming the cache is always warm when sizing the database. Restarting all cache nodes or all app servers at the same time.
</div>

